{"id":"cluster-administration-observability","corpus":"k8s","title":"Observability","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/observability/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Observability"},{"kind":"p","text":"Understand how to gain end-to-end visibility of a Kubernetes cluster through the collection of metrics, logs, and traces."},{"kind":"p","text":"In Kubernetes, observability is the process of collecting and analyzing metrics, logs, and traces—often referred to as the three pillars of observability—in order to obtain a better understanding of the internal state, performance, and health of the cluster."},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes control plane components, as well as many add-ons, generate and emit these signals. By aggregating and correlating them, you can gain a unified picture of the control plane, add-ons, and applications across the cluster."},{"kind":"p","text":"Figure 1 outlines how cluster components emit the three primary signal types."},{"kind":"p","text":"Figure 1. High-level signals emitted by cluster components and their consumers."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Metrics"},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes components emit metrics in Prometheus format from their /metrics endpoints, including:"},{"kind":"li","text":"kube-controller-manager"},{"kind":"li","text":"kube-proxy"},{"kind":"li","text":"kube-apiserver"},{"kind":"li","text":"kube-scheduler"},{"kind":"li","text":"kubelet"},{"kind":"p","text":"The kubelet also exposes metrics at /metrics/cadvisor, /metrics/resource, and /metrics/probes, and add-ons such as kube-state-metrics enrich those control plane signals with Kubernetes object status."},{"kind":"p","text":"A typical Kubernetes metrics pipeline periodically scrapes these endpoints and stores the samples in a time series database (for example with Prometheus)."},{"kind":"p","text":"See the system metrics guide for details and configuration options."},{"kind":"p","text":"Figure 2 outlines a common Kubernetes metrics pipeline."},{"kind":"p","text":"Figure 2. Components of a typical Kubernetes metrics pipeline."},{"kind":"p","text":"For multi-cluster or multi-cloud visibility, distributed time series databases (for example Thanos or Cortex) can complement Prometheus."},{"kind":"p","text":"See Common observability tools - metrics tools for metrics scrapers and time series databases."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Metrics API"},{"kind":"p","text":"The Kubernetes Metrics API provides CPU and memory resource usage for nodes and Pods. The kubectl top command and components such as the HorizontalPodAutoscaler and VerticalPodAutoscaler use this API."},{"kind":"p","text":"kubectl top supports both metrics.k8s.io/v1 and metrics.k8s.io/v1beta1. It prefers v1 when that version is available and falls back to v1beta1. In Kubernetes v1.37, the HorizontalPodAutoscaler controller supports only metrics.k8s.io/v1beta1; support for metrics.k8s.io/v1 is planned but is not available yet."},{"kind":"p","text":"Unlike the component metrics endpoints described earlier, the Metrics API is served through the Kubernetes API aggregation layer. Your cluster must run Metrics Server or another implementation that provides the API. The Metrics API intentionally provides only the resource metrics needed for autoscaling and basic inspection; it is not a replacement for a full monitoring pipeline."},{"kind":"p","text":"To learn about the API, its implementations, and the data flow from kubelets to its clients, see the resource metrics pipeline."},{"kind":"h3","text":"See Also"},{"kind":"li","text":"System metrics for Kubernetes components"},{"kind":"li","text":"Resource usage monitoring with metrics-server"},{"kind":"li","text":"kube-state-metrics concept"},{"kind":"h2","text":"Logs"},{"kind":"p","text":"Logs provide a chronological record of events inside applications, Kubernetes system components, and security-related activities such as audit logging."},{"kind":"p","text":"Container runtimes capture a containerized application’s output from standard output (stdout) and standard error (stderr) streams. While runtimes implement this differently, the integration with the kubelet is standardized through the CRI logging format, and the kubelet makes these logs available through kubectl logs."},{"kind":"p","text":"Figure 3a. Node-level logging architecture."},{"kind":"p","text":"System component logs capture events from the cluster and are often useful for debugging and troubleshooting. These components are classified in two different ways: those that run in a container and those that do not. For example, the kube-scheduler and kube-proxy usually run in containers, whereas the kubelet and the container runtime run directly on the host."},{"kind":"li","text":"On machines with systemd, the kubelet and container runtime write to journald. Otherwise, they write to .log files in the /var/log directory."},{"kind":"li","text":"System components that run inside containers always write to .log files in /var/log, bypassing the default container logging mechanism."},{"kind":"p","text":"System component and container logs stored under /var/log require log rotation to prevent uncontrolled growth. Some cluster provisioning scripts install log rotation by default; verify your environment and adjust as needed. See the system logs reference for details on locations, formats, and configuration options."},{"kind":"p","text":"Most clusters run a node-level logging agent (for example, Fluent Bit or Fluentd) that tails these files and forwards entries to a central log store. The logging architecture guidance explains how to design such pipelines, apply retention, and log flows to backends."},{"kind":"p","text":"Figure 3 outlines a common log aggregation pipeline."}],"lemmas":{"observabilities":["observabilities","observability"],"understands":["understands","understand"],"und